meta {
  name: Check integrations compatibility - incompatible version
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/opencti/integrations-compatibility?integration_versions=mitreattack@6.240930.0&version=6.200101.0
  body: none
  auth: none
}

params:query {
  integration_versions: mitreattack@6.240930.0
  version: 6.200101.0
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
}

docs {
  An incompatible pair is an *answer*, not an error: the response is still
  200, with `compatible: false` and an `incompatibility_reason` on the
  offending entry. One bad entry never hides the verdict of the others.

  {
    "opencti_version": "6.200101.0",
    "integrations": [
      {
        "slug": "mitreattack",
        "integration_version": "6.240930.0",
        "compatible": false,
        "incompatibility_reason": "opencti_version_too_old",
        "minimum_deployable_version": "6.240101.0",
        "latest_compatible_integration_version": "6.230101.0"
      }
    ]
  }

  `incompatibility_reason` is one of:
    - opencti_version_too_old       the connector needs a newer OpenCTI
    - lts_track_mismatch            LTS connector on non-LTS OpenCTI, or vice versa
    - integration_decommissioned    that version exists but is retired
    - unknown_integration_version   the slug is a connector, but not at that version
    - unknown_integration_slug      no integration carries that slug
    - integration_type_not_supported  the slug is not a connector

  The last three come back *without* `compatible`: nothing was verified, so
  neither true nor false would be honest. `false` would block a deployment
  that may well be fine, `true` would vouch for something never checked.

  `latest_compatible_integration_version` is the newest version of that slug
  that does work with the resolved OpenCTI version, so the caller gets the
  fix along with the diagnosis. It is absent when no version of that slug is
  compatible at all.
}
